#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060662} :activity/id #uuid "57e9a8cd-9225-5fac-965e-807daa0874c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151 :topic/id #uuid "deb0c389-354c-501a-95ee-3e33842eb21f" :topic/math-academy-id 155} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060935 :tutorial/content "Remember that we can represent a system of two linear equations in two variables using matrices. We can do the same thing for a system of three linear equations in three variables, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y\\text{ }-\\text{ }2z=7 \\\\ 2x\\text{ }-\\text{ }1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }4z=2 \\\\ 2x+1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }3z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a $3 \\times 3$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $3 \\times 3$ coefficient matrix:\n\n![](images/40/40541d2ecb10c763056ae72734e723a9d5967ed9accaf0bbde6f2a2f9ec96b65.svg)\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $3 \\times 1$ column vectors\n\n![](images/e5/e56ed72d2d17f9a4bc59bfe51a96534a72ebe481a312b8e9c6c028ade96d3748.svg)\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n![](images/f0/f06e5a54e106bcdca8b5cbad677ade3476ba8f6218b4aa79d196f54e0fff3e2c.svg)\n\nWe can write any system of three equations with three variables in this form. Just to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3x+2y\\text{ }-\\text{ }2z \\\\ 2x\\text{ }-\\text{ }1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }4z \\\\ 2x+1y\\text{ }-\\text{ }3z\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =b\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "93427b42-9f68-58e3-a3fd-19c5c8c58d90" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3053 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "944c9195-2d49-5b9d-bc5c-1cd47e01dc13" :step/math-academy-id 16480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060663}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059566 :question/id #uuid "7bfcfcbb-6212-5f9b-90aa-c79fbf7221e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4518"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1938c5da-d01a-53b7-a811-9afe1e43d152" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426758}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Entries of a Matrix Product Representing a 3x3 System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "c9335040-4892-53be-90b2-54e91913879f" :step/math-academy-id 16482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059567 :question/id #uuid "c4af8b0d-e4ee-56f4-9cf6-594530639339" :question/math-academy-id "e-4519"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7958ef60-8041-535b-bff8-b35e84c54412" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059953} {:db/id #edb/r